# Blobs already published in a pushed commit, accepted as-is.
#
# Keyed by exact blob SHA. That is deliberate: a baselined SHA pins one
# specific piece of content that is already public and cannot be recalled.
# Anything NEW produces a different SHA and fails the run, so baselining the
# past does not blind this check to the future.
#
# Why baseline rather than rewrite history. These blobs hold company names in
# test fixtures, one illustrative comment, and one note recording a decision,
# all pushed and public. Rewriting pushed history would break every clone and
# still would not remove them: the objects stay addressable by SHA on the
# forge until it chooses to collect them, so the remediation is incomplete as
# well as expensive. Against that, a check that exits non-zero forever teaches
# you to ignore it, and an ignored check is worse than no check.
#
# Every one of these paths is clean from that commit onward. This file records
# what was already out, not a standing permission to add more.
#
# Format: <sha>  <path>  <reason>

